<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-4">
                <div><img src="~/assets/img/dynamics365/businesswoman-discussing-with-disabled-woman.webp" class="img-fluid text-start ms-0 mb-5 me-3 p-3" alt="Business stakeholders discussing a Dynamics 365 implementation" /></div>
                <div><img src="~/assets/img/dynamics365/representation-user-experience-interface-design-computer.webp" class="img-fluid text-start ms-0 mb-5 mt-3 me-3 p-3" alt="User experience design for a Dynamics 365 business application" /></div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-lg-8 Imgborder" align="justify">
                <h2><b>Common Pitfalls in Dynamics 365 Implementation and How to Avoid Them</b></h2>
                <p>
                    A well-planned Dynamics 365 rollout reduces avoidable rework and helps teams adopt the new processes. The following implementation risks are important to address during discovery, configuration, migration, testing, and go-live preparation.
                </p>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Lack of Clear Business Requirements: Failing to define business needs up front can lead to missed opportunities or wasted resources. Map specific requirements and success measures for each department before configuring Dynamics 365.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Overlooking Data Quality: Migrating poor-quality data can create issues in the new system. Profile, cleanse, map, and validate data before migration to protect accuracy and data integrity.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Underestimating User Training: Without role-relevant training, users may struggle to adopt new Dynamics 365 processes. Prepare practical guidance for the people who will use the system every day.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Insufficient Change Management: Introducing new workflows without managing change can result in resistance. Prepare teams with clear communication, stakeholder involvement, and support throughout the transition.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Not Preparing for Post-Implementation: The work does not end at go-live. Define how users can receive support, how issues are reviewed, and how the Dynamics 365 solution will be optimized as needs change.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul><br />
                <h2><b>The Importance of Customization in Dynamics 365 Implementation</b></h2>
                <p>Dynamics 365 provides broad standard functionality, while thoughtful configuration and customization can align the platform with unique business needs. iFOX uses this distinction to keep implementation decisions focused on usability, maintainability, and business value.</p>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Align Customizations with Business Processes: Ensure that configuration and customization reflect specific organizational workflows so the solution supports efficient work and user adoption.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Scalable Customization: Make implementation decisions with future maintenance and changing business requirements in mind, so the Dynamics 365 environment remains practical to manage.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Workflows and Automations: Use appropriate Dynamics 365 workflows and Power Platform automation to reduce repetitive tasks, improve consistency, and support more efficient processes.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Reports and Dashboards: Shape reporting views around roles and business objectives so teams can review relevant information and make more informed decisions.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  User Feedback: Involve users in configuration reviews, gather feedback, and refine the solution so Dynamics 365 supports real daily tasks rather than theoretical processes.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
